#env
.env
.env.*
# ...but the TEMPLATE must be committed — it is the only place the required
# variable names are written down. Without this negation `.env.*` above swallows
# it and a new deploy has nothing to copy. Verify with:
#   git check-ignore -v .env.example   ->  must print nothing
!.env.example
# ...and so must the PRODUCTION file. It holds exactly two PUBLIC feature flags
# (VITE_USE_LOCAL_AUTH, VITE_STANDALONE_LOCAL). Vite folds every VITE_-prefixed
# variable into the shipped JavaScript, so those values are readable by anyone who
# loads the site — ignoring the file bought no secrecy. What it did buy was two
# dead deploys: Cloudflare's Git build and GitHub Actions clone this repo, saw
# neither flag, and produced a bundle that loads and can never log anybody in
# (Cloudflare builds #2576feba and #159d05dc, 2026-08-24). With base44 retired
# those two flags describe the ONLY deployment shape that works, so they belong
# with the code, not in a hosting dashboard that has to be kept correct by hand.
# NEVER put a secret in that file — scripts/probe-standalone-deploy.mjs section 6
# fails if any other key appears in it. Verify with:
#   git check-ignore -v .env.production   ->  must print nothing
!.env.production

